{"pistudioPack":1,"kind":"help","title":"Help","version":"2026-09-29 11:20","minAppVersion":"0.9.22","files":{"01 Getting started/01 Welcome.md":"# Welcome to PiStudio\n\nPiStudio engraves cups, tumblers and glasses on galvo and CO2 lasers with **PiBurn** rotaries — and\nlets you set the rotary exactly the way your hardware needs it.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Press **F1** anywhere to open or close this help. Type in the search box at the top to find a\n> page, or use the contents on the left.\n\n## The short version\n\n1. **Add your laser** — pick it from the machine library and give it a name. See [Add your laser](add-your-laser).\n2. **Choose your rotary** — PiBurn Galvo Grip, Grip 2, a roller… See [Choose your rotary](choose-your-rotary).\n3. **Set the cup diameter** — the one number PiStudio really needs. Pick the cup from the\n   Drinkware library and even that is filled in for you.\n4. **Import your artwork**, check it on the **Cup preview**, press **Preview**, then **Send**.\n\nThe whole walk-through is in [Your first engraving](first-engraving).\n\n## Where things are\n\n| Area | What it holds |\n|---|---|\n| Top bar | New, Open, Save, Undo · your laser · Frame, Preview, Send · Rotary setup · Cup preview · Help · Settings |\n| Left toolbar | Select, Text, Rectangle, Ellipse, Import, Fit view, Turn view |\n| Canvas | The cup unrolled — the dashed red lines are the seam |\n| Right panel | **Layers** (mode, speed, power), **Rotary**, **Machine** |\n| Colour strip | The layer colours — click one to put the selection on that layer |\n\n## Getting help\n\nLensDigital updates this help without a new PiStudio (Settings → **Libraries & help**). For\nsupport, visit [lensdigital.com](https://lensdigital.com/); the PiBurn manuals are at\n[piburn.info/manual](http://piburn.info/manual).\n","01 Getting started/02 Add your laser.md":"---\nid: add-your-laser\n---\n# Add your laser\n\nPiStudio calls each laser you work with a **device**: a name you choose, what kind of laser it is,\nand how to reach it. Several lasers are fine — pick the one you are using in the top bar.\n\n![The device form with the machine library](../images/device-form@2x.webp)\n\n## Add one\n\n1. In the top bar, open the machine list and choose **Add a machine…** (or **Manage devices…** →\n   **Add device**).\n2. **Name** — anything that tells your machines apart (\"Shop CO2\", \"Vertigo\").\n3. **Your laser — from the machine library** — choose the **type**, then the **make** and the\n   **model**. PiStudio then knows which driver runs it and fills in what is known about it.\n   Not listed? Leave it on **Not listed** and pick the **Machine type** (the controller) yourself:\n   *Ruida (Ethernet)* for most CO2 lasers, *JCZ fiber galvo (EZCad2)*, *BSL galvo*, or your xTool model.\n4. The **address**:\n   - **xTool** — over USB the laser is at `201.234.3.1`; on Wi-Fi use the address it reports.\n     USB on Windows needs the RNDIS driver — see [Cannot connect over USB](usb-driver).\n   - **Ruida** — the same IP address LightBurn uses, or **USB** (a serial port).\n   - **Fiber and UV galvos** — nothing to type: they are found on USB.\n5. **Save**. The new laser is selected straight away.\n\n## Laser details (optional)\n\n| Field | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| **Laser power** (W) | Shown under the power dial: \"100% = 60 W\". A laser with two sources (xTool F1: blue and IR) gets one box per source. |\n| **Top speed** (mm/s) | The end of the speed dial's scale. Faster speeds can still be typed — PiStudio only warns. |\n| **Rotary driver — steps per motor turn** | The microstep setting of the laser's rotary stepper driver (for example 12800). Times a rotary's gear ratio it gives the steps per rotation PiStudio suggests — see [Steps per rotation and gear ratio](steps-per-rotation). Not used on an xTool, which turns the rotary in millimetres per rotation. |\n\nPicked from the library, these are filled in for you, and they stay yours to change. Leave any of\nthem empty if you don't know it.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> A Ruida laser also asks for its **origin corner** — the same question LightBurn's device wizard\n> asks. If engravings ever come out mirrored, this is the setting to check.\n\n## Share your device list\n\n**Export** writes your devices to a file; **Import** reads one — handy for a second computer.\n","01 Getting started/03 Connect over USB or Wi-Fi.md":"---\nid: connect\n---\n# Connect over USB or Wi-Fi\n\nHow PiStudio reaches a laser depends on its controller.\n\n## xTool F-series\n\n- **USB** — the laser appears as a *network adapter* (not a COM port) and answers at\n  `201.234.3.1`. **On Windows this needs the RNDIS driver**, which Windows 11 no longer installs\n  by itself. If you hear the USB chime but PiStudio finds nothing, follow\n  [Cannot connect over USB](usb-driver) before trying other cables.\n- **Wi-Fi** — set the laser up on your network once (xTool Studio can do it), close Studio, then\n  use **Scan again** in PiStudio's connect window.\n\nThe connection chip in the top bar shows the state; click it for the connect window.\n\n## Ruida (most CO2 lasers)\n\nUse the laser's IP address — the same one LightBurn uses — or USB. PiStudio reads the bed size and\nthe rotary settings from the controller when it connects.\n\n## Fiber and UV galvos (EZCad2 and BSL boards)\n\nThese are driven over USB and found by themselves; the **Machine** tab on the right shows whether\nthe board is there. The laser must be switched on for its board to appear.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> Only one program can drive a laser at a time. Close LightBurn, xTool Studio or EZCad before\n> connecting PiStudio.\n","01 Getting started/04 Choose your rotary.md":"---\nid: choose-your-rotary\n---\n# Choose your rotary\n\nOpen **Rotary setup** from the top bar and press **Change rotary**. The picker shows every rotary\nPiStudio knows — the current PiBurn line first, then retired PiBurn models, your own, and the\ngeneric ones.\n\n- **Chuck** rotaries (Galvo Grip, Grip 2, Grip Lite) hold the cup in jaws and turn it directly.\n- **Roller** rotaries (PiBurn V, PiBurn Lite) turn the cup by contact on two wheels. For a roller,\n  the number that matters is the **roller diameter**, not the cup's.\n- **Omni** models are both: one card, and a **Type** switch in Rotary setup moves between the chuck\n  and the roller end.\n\nEach PiBurn card has **Manual** and **Buy** buttons.\n\n## Something that is not listed\n\n**Create custom rotary** in the picker: a name, chuck or roller, the roller diameter, and — if you\nknow it — the **gear ratio** (motor turns for one turn of the chuck or of the rollers). With the\ngear ratio and your laser's rotary steps per turn, PiStudio suggests the steps per rotation; see\n[Steps per rotation and gear ratio](steps-per-rotation).\n\n## Then: the cup\n\nSet the **cup diameter** — or pick the cup from the **Drinkware library**, which fills in the\ndiameter, height, shape and any keep-out zones. That is all a chuck needs. More in\n[Rotary setup](rotary-setup).\n","01 Getting started/05 Your first engraving.md":"---\nid: first-engraving\n---\n# Your first engraving\n\nA complete run, start to finish.\n\n1. **Pick your laser** in the top bar. First time? See [Add your laser](add-your-laser).\n2. **Rotary setup** — choose the rotary and set the cup (Drinkware library, or type the diameter).\n3. **Import** your artwork (**Ctrl+I**): pictures, SVG, Illustrator files, LightBurn files or DXF.\n   Or add words with the **Text** tool.\n4. **Place it.** The canvas is the cup unrolled: one way is along the cup, the other is around it.\n   The dashed red lines are the seam; shaded bands are areas to keep clear (handles, logos).\n5. **Layers** — each colour is a layer with its own **mode** (Score, Fill, Image), **speed** and\n   **power**. Turn the dials, or type the numbers. See [Layers, speed and power](layers).\n6. **Check it on the cup** — the **Cup preview** shows the design wrapped on the real shape.\n7. **Frame** to see where the laser will go (the rotary turns during the frame).\n8. **Preview** (**Ctrl+P**) generates the job and shows exactly what will run, and how long it takes.\n9. **Send**. PiStudio reports progress; **Stop** is always in the top bar, and **Esc** stops too.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Not sure about power and speed on a new material? The **Test grid** engraves a grid of\n> power/speed pairs; save the winner as a material so it is one click next time — see\n> [Materials](materials).\n","02 Designing/01 The workspace.md":"---\nid: workspace\n---\n# The workspace\n\n## The canvas is the cup, unrolled\n\nWith a rotary on, the canvas is the cup's surface laid flat: one direction runs along the cup (rim\nto base), the other runs around it. Its length around is the cup's circumference, so a design that\nfills it wraps all the way round.\n\n- The **dashed red lines** are the seam — where the wrap starts and ends.\n- **base** and **rim** are marked at the ends, so you always know which way up the design lands.\n- **Shaded bands** are areas to keep clear: a handle, a maker's logo, a bare metal ring. They come\n  from the cup you picked in the Drinkware library.\n\n## Tools\n\n| Tool | Key | Use |\n|---|---|---|\n| Select | V | Pick, move, resize and rotate. Shift keeps proportions (or frees them — see the padlock). |\n| Text | T | Type words in any installed font. |\n| Rectangle | R | Draw a box. |\n| Ellipse | E | Draw a circle or oval. |\n| Import | Ctrl+I | Bring in artwork — see [Importing artwork](importing-artwork). |\n| Fit view | Ctrl+0 | Show the whole work area. |\n| Turn view | | Turn the workspace a quarter turn, to match how the cup sits on your laser. |\n\nArrow keys nudge the selection 0.1 mm (Shift: 1 mm). **Ctrl+D** duplicates, **Delete** removes.\nRight-click the canvas for more (align, group, arrange).\n\n## Layers are colours\n\nEvery shape belongs to a layer, and each layer is one colour on the colour strip under the canvas.\nSelect shapes and click a colour (or press 1…9, 0) to move them onto that layer. The layer's\nsettings — mode, speed, power — are in the **Layers** tab. See [Layers, speed and power](layers).\n\n## Check the result on the cup\n\nThe **Cup preview** (top bar) shows the design wrapped on the real cup shape, with its colour and\nfinish. Enlarge it for a big view; **Render mockup…** there makes a picture you can send a customer.\n","02 Designing/02 Importing artwork.md":"---\nid: importing-artwork\n---\n# Importing artwork\n\n**Import** (left toolbar, or **Ctrl+I**) — or simply drag files onto the window.\n\n| File | What you get |\n|---|---|\n| **Pictures** (PNG, JPG, BMP, GIF, WebP) | An image to engrave in *Image* mode — dithered to dots. |\n| **SVG** | Shapes, kept as vectors. Colours become layers. |\n| **Illustrator** (AI, EPS, PDF) | Vectors from the file; colours become layers. |\n| **LightBurn** (.lbrn, .lbrn2) | Its vectors, with the layer settings. |\n| **DXF** | CAD drawings; PiStudio asks for the units if the file doesn't say. |\n\n## Colours become layers\n\nVector artwork keeps its colours: each colour lands on its own layer, so a two-colour logo becomes\ntwo layers you can set separately — engrave one, leave the other. Change a shape's layer by\nselecting it and clicking another colour on the strip.\n\n## Size and place\n\nImported art arrives at its own size where the file gives one. Use the handles, or type the size\nand position in the bar above the canvas. The padlock there keeps proportions.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> For a full wrap made in another program, the **PiStudio Wrap Designer** exports SVG/DXF sized\n> to your cup.\n","02 Designing/03 Layers, speed and power.md":"---\nid: layers\n---\n# Layers, speed and power\n\nClick a layer in the **Layers** tab to see its engraving settings.\n\n![Speed and power dials](../images/dials@2x.webp)\n\n## Mode\n\n| Mode | What the laser does |\n|---|---|\n| **Score** (Line) | Traces the outlines. Not available on a roller rotary. |\n| **Fill** | Scans back and forth inside closed shapes. |\n| **Image** | Engraves a picture as dots (dithering). |\n\n## Speed and power dials\n\n- **Turn a dial** by dragging the knob up or down (hold **Shift** for fine steps), or press on the\n  scale and drag round it. Click a dial and the arrow keys, Page Up/Down and the mouse wheel work too.\n- **Type an exact number** in the box beside it.\n- The **speed dial** runs to your laser's top speed. Its colours show the comfortable range, the\n  range to use with care, and the last stretch before the top. Any speed can be typed — past the\n  end the needle stops and a warning says so. Set your laser's top speed in Devices (Laser details);\n  without it the dial assumes a typical figure for the machine type (1000 mm/s for a Ruida CO2).\n- The **power dial** is percent of the laser's full power, with a **red zone from 80 %**. With the\n  laser's wattage set in Devices it also shows watts (\"100% = 60 W\"); a two-source laser (blue + IR,\n  blue + fiber) shows the watts of the layer's source.\n- The **numbers** turn from their usual colour to red as a setting nears the red zone.\n- The colours of the speed dial are explained under its (i): green is the comfortable range,\n  yellow is for care, red is the last stretch before the laser's top speed.\n- On a **Ruida** laser, power is a *Max / Min* pair as in LightBurn: **Show MIN power** opens\n  the minimum (used in corners). The link button makes Min follow Max.\n- On a **UV galvo** there is no power: its dial turns the **Q-pulse** (the pulse width, in ns),\n  which with the **Frequency** sets the energy. (The xTool F2 Ultra UV does use power %, like xTool\n  Studio.)\n\nPrefer plain number fields? Settings → General → **Show speed and power as dials**.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> With sounds on, turning the power dial up spins up a reactor, and it holds at the level you stop\n> at until the pointer leaves the dial. Passing the pointer over it makes no sound. Settings → **Sounds**.\n\n## Line interval and DPI\n\nThe same setting two ways: the spacing of the scan lines (mm), or lines per inch. Finer lines give\na denser mark and take longer.\n\n## Advanced Settings\n\nScan angle (or *Start from Top / Bottom* on a CO2), bidirectional scanning, cross-hatch, invert,\nfrequency on fiber sources, and **Passes** — how many times the layer runs.\n\n## Materials\n\nThe **Material** list fills a layer from a saved recipe; the disk button saves this layer's\nnumbers as a new one. See [Materials](materials).\n","02 Designing/04 Materials.md":"---\nid: materials\n---\n# Materials\n\nA material is a saved layer recipe — mode, speed, power, line interval, passes — for one machine\ntype. Picking one in a layer's **Material** list copies its numbers into the layer.\n\n- **Save a material**: set a layer the way you like it and press the disk button next to the list.\n  Tick **Quick pick** to put it at the top of the list (★).\n- **Edited** appears when a layer's numbers no longer match its material: **update** writes your\n  changes into the material, **revert** takes the material's numbers back.\n- **Manage** them in Settings → **Materials**: rename, delete, import and export (to share with\n  another computer or a customer).\n\n## Finding the numbers: the test grid\n\nA **Test grid** engraves a grid of squares, each at a different power and speed. Engrave it on a\nspare piece of the material, pick the square you like best, and save those numbers as a material.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Materials belong to a machine type: the same numbers on a different laser can mark very\n> differently.\n","03 Rotary/01 Rotary setup.md":"---\nid: rotary-setup\n---\n# Rotary setup\n\n**Rotary setup** (top bar) holds everything about the rotary and the cup on it.\n\n## The one number: the diameter\n\nFor a **chuck**, set the cup's **diameter** where the design sits — or pick the cup from the\nDrinkware library. That is the whole setup: height, taper and the rest only make the preview\ntruer.\n\nFor a **roller**, PiStudio asks for the **roller diameter** instead: the rollers turn the cup by\ncontact, so a millimetre of cup surface is a millimetre of roller surface, whatever the cup's size.\nPiBurn rollers come with their diameter filled in; rubber wears, so you can type the one you\nmeasure — **Default** puts the published figure back.\n\n## How the cup is mounted\n\nPick how the cup sits (held at the rim or at the base; on the VertiGo, upright or upside-down).\nPiStudio turns the job to match, so the design lands the right way up. The picture shows the\nmounting you picked.\n\n## Travel per rotation\n\n- **xTool lasers** move the rotary in *millimetres per rotation*. PiBurn rotaries come with the\n  figure LensDigital measured (the Galvo Grip is 119.7 on the F1 Lite, F2 and F2 Ultra UV). For\n  anything else, run the calibration — Settings → **Rotary calibration** — once per rotary and laser.\n- **Ruida lasers** convert rotary moves themselves from their stored diameter and **steps per\n  rotation**. PiStudio keeps the steps for each rotary on each laser and writes them with\n  **Update machine**. See [Steps per rotation and gear ratio](steps-per-rotation).\n- **Fiber galvos** (EZCad2) use **Pulses / turn** in the Machine tab — the same idea.\n\n## After the job\n\n**Return to start** turns the cup slowly back to where the job began, ready for the next one;\n**Stay at end** leaves it where it stopped.\n","03 Rotary/02 Steps per rotation and gear ratio.md":"---\nid: steps-per-rotation\n---\n# Steps per rotation and gear ratio\n\nA controller that drives the rotary in motor steps (Ruida CO2 lasers, EZCad2 fiber galvos) must\nknow how many steps make **one full turn of the cup** — for a roller, one turn of the rollers.\n\n## Where the number comes from\n\n    steps per rotation = rotary driver steps per motor turn × gear ratio\n\n- **Rotary driver steps per motor turn** (SPR) is a property of the **laser**: the microstep\n  setting of the stepper driver its rotary port uses — often 1600, 3200, 6400, 10000 or 12800.\n  Set it in Devices → your laser → **Laser details**. Lasers from the machine library may bring it\n  with them.\n- **Gear ratio** is a property of the **rotary**: how many turns the motor makes for one turn of\n  the chuck (or of the rollers). PiBurn rotaries carry it in the rotary library; for your own,\n  type it when you create the custom rotary.\n\nWhen both are known, Rotary setup shows **Suggested 48000 (12800 × 3.75)** under the steps field,\nwith a **Use** button. Nothing changes until you press it.\n\n| Example | SPR | Gear ratio | Steps per rotation |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| PiBurn Grip 2 on a fiber galvo | 12800 | 3.75 | 48000 |\n| PiBurn Grip 1 on a laser whose driver is set to 3200 | 3200 | 1 | 3200 |\n| PiBurn V1–V4 or PiBurn Lite on the same laser | 3200 | 2.5 | 8000 |\n\nPiBurn gear ratios: Grip 2, Galvo Grip, Grip Lite and the Omni 2's grip **3.75**; Grip 1 and the\nOmni 1's grip **1**; PiBurn V1–V4, the Omni 1's rollers and PiBurn Lite **2.5**; PiBurn V and the\nOmni 2's rollers **4.5**.\n\n## Thunder lasers\n\nOn a **Thunder** laser the rotary's stepper driver is built into the motor:\n\n- a **Grip 2** (and its family) is always **30000** steps per rotation there, a **Grip 1 8000** —\n  PiStudio suggests exactly that;\n- a **PiBurn V** or the **Omni 2's rollers** gear **3.6** there instead of 4.5 — PiStudio multiplies\n  the motor's steps per turn (Devices → Laser details) by 3.6.\n\nPick your Thunder from the machine library in Devices so PiStudio knows it is one.\n\n## Writing it to a Ruida\n\nPiStudio keeps the steps for each rotary **on each laser** (the same rotary on two lasers can need\ndifferent numbers). **Update machine** writes them — with the rotary on/off and the diameter — to\nthe controller, the way LightBurn's rotary setup does. Sending a job sets only the diameter.\n\n> [!WARNING]\n> A wrong steps-per-rotation number makes the design too long or too short around the cup. Check a\n> new setup with a quick frame, or a small engraving and a mark on the cup, before a full job.\n","03 Rotary/03 Calibrating a rotary.md":"---\nid: calibration\n---\n# Calibrating a rotary\n\nOn an **xTool** laser PiStudio scales the rotary itself, using the rotary's *millimetres per\nrotation* on that laser. PiBurn rotaries ship with LensDigital's measured figures where they exist;\nanything else is calibrated once, and the result is kept for that rotary on that laser.\n\nOpen Settings → **Rotary calibration** (connected to the laser).\n\n1. **Mark the start** — mount a cup, set its diameter in Rotary setup, and draw a small mark across\n   chuck and cup (a pen line over both is enough).\n2. **Rotate one full turn** — press the button. The laser stays off; only the rotary turns.\n3. **Where did your mark end up?**\n   - **Dead on** — the marks line up again: your figure is right.\n   - **Short** or **Past** — measure the gap *along the cup's surface* (wrap a strip of paper\n     round the cup, mark both points, measure it flat) and type it in. PiStudio shows the\n     corrected figure; **Apply & save to preset**.\n4. Run another full turn to check. One or two rounds usually lands it dead on.\n\n## Good to know\n\n- A calibration belongs to the **rotary and laser pair**: the same rotary on another laser keeps\n  its own number.\n- Check on a second cup of a different diameter: if both come out right, the figure is a true\n  property of the rotary.\n- On a **Ruida** laser the controller converts rotary moves itself, so there the figure is only a\n  cross-check — what matters is the steps per rotation, see\n  [Steps per rotation and gear ratio](steps-per-rotation).\n","04 Drinkware/01 Drinkware library.md":"---\nid: drinkware-library\n---\n# Drinkware library\n\nThe Drinkware library holds the cups you engrave — tumblers, mugs, wine glasses, bottles, buckets —\nwith their real measurements. Picking one fills in the cup for you: diameter, height, shape, rings,\nhandle, colour, and the shaded **keep-out zones** (a handle, a maker's logo).\n\n## Using it\n\n- Open it from **Rotary setup** (the cup). Search by name, brand or kind.\n- Click a cup to use it. Every number stays editable afterwards; the cup on the canvas never\n  changes the library by itself.\n- **Update** writes the cup you are working on back into its library entry.\n- **Buy** (on cups that have a link) opens the shop page in your browser.\n\n## Your own cups\n\n- **Edit** or duplicate any cup, or create a new one in the **Drinkware editor**: name, brand,\n  kind, glass or steel, colour, the measurements from base to rim, rings, handle and zones.\n- **Buy link** (optional) — a shop address for the cup. It shows as a **Buy** button in the\n  library, handy for re-ordering blanks or sending a customer to the product.\n- The **Cup Designer** (top bar) is the full editor for shapes and handles, with a 3D view.\n\n## Sharing\n\n**Export** writes the whole library to one file; a cup's download button exports just that cup,\nwith its models. **Select cups** exports a few at once. **Open** replaces the active collection;\n**Add** merges a file into it.\n","04 Drinkware/02 Cup preview and mockups.md":"---\nid: cup-preview\n---\n# Cup preview and mockups\n\nThe **Cup preview** (top bar) shows your design wrapped on the cup — its shape, colour, finish,\nrings and handle — and updates as you edit.\n\n- **Drag** to turn the cup, **scroll** to zoom. The play button spins it.\n- **Steel / Glass** and the colour dots change the look (display only — the job is not affected).\n- The enlarge button opens a big view.\n\n## Mockups for customers\n\nIn the big view, **Render mockup…** makes a picture of the engraved cup on a studio background —\nup to 4K, with shadows, and for glass a drink inside (wine, water, coffee) that makes the frosted\nengraving stand out. Save it and send it to a customer before you engrave.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> The preview never changes the job: it is a picture of what the laser will do, not a setting.\n","05 Machines/01 xTool F-series.md":"---\nid: xtool\n---\n# xTool F-series\n\nPiStudio drives the xTool F1, F1 Lite, F1 Ultra, F2, F2 Ultra and F2 Ultra UV galvo lasers.\n\n## Connecting\n\n- **USB**: the laser is a network adapter at `201.234.3.1` — on Windows install the RNDIS driver\n  first ([Cannot connect over USB](usb-driver)).\n- **Wi-Fi**: once the laser is on your network, **Scan again** in the connect window finds it.\n\n## Things that are different on these lasers\n\n- The rotary is scaled by PiStudio in **millimetres per rotation**. The PiBurn Galvo Grip is\n  119.7 mm per rotation on the F1 Lite, F2 and F2 Ultra UV — measured by LensDigital.\n- The **laser module** (blue, IR, fiber, UV) is chosen per layer on lasers that have more than one.\n- The **F2 Ultra UV** marks glass with its pulsed UV source; a layer's **Frequency** is part of\n  its recipe (40 kHz is xTool Studio's default).\n- **Frame** turns the cup during the frame, so you see the whole wrap.\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> If the laser stops moving but still answers — a banner in PiStudio says so — power it off and on.\n> See [The laser stopped responding](laser-not-responding).\n","05 Machines/02 Ruida lasers.md":"---\nid: ruida\n---\n# Ruida lasers (most CO2 lasers)\n\nMost CO2 lasers — Thunder, Aeon, Boss, OMTech and many more — have a **Ruida** controller.\nPiStudio talks to it over Ethernet or USB, like LightBurn, and any rotary can be used on it.\n\n## Set up\n\n1. Add the laser in Devices (from the machine library, or *Ruida (Ethernet)* if it is not listed).\n2. Enter the **IP address** LightBurn uses — or choose **USB** and the serial port.\n3. Set the **origin corner** the same as in LightBurn's device wizard.\n4. Connect. PiStudio reads the bed size and the rotary settings from the controller.\n\n## Sending a job\n\n**Send** writes the job to the controller's memory under a file name; start it on the machine's\npanel. PiStudio remembers the name you gave each design.\n\n## Rotary on a Ruida\n\nThe controller turns the rotary itself, from its stored **diameter** and **steps per rotation**:\n\n- For a **chuck** the diameter is the cup's; for a **roller** it is the roller's.\n- The steps per rotation are kept for each rotary on each laser; **Update machine** writes them.\n  See [Steps per rotation and gear ratio](steps-per-rotation).\n\n## Power: Max and Min\n\nA Ruida layer has LightBurn's Max / Min power pair. **Show MIN power** opens the minimum; the link\nmakes it follow Max.\n","05 Machines/03 Fiber and UV galvos.md":"---\nid: galvo\n---\n# Fiber and UV galvos\n\nPiStudio drives fiber galvos on **EZCad2 (JCZ)** boards and UV galvos on **BSL (SeaCad)** boards\n— for example the ComMarker Omni X UV — straight over USB.\n\n## Set up\n\n- Add the laser in Devices. There is no address: the board is found on USB when the laser is on.\n- A fiber laser's calibration (field size, galvo scale, timing) is part of its device. If you\n  already use it with LightBurn, its figures are the ones to enter.\n- The **Machine** tab on the right shows the board, framing and the burn controls.\n\n## Burning\n\n**Send** opens the burn window with the job's size and time; the burn starts when you confirm it.\n**Stop** (or **Esc**) stops it.\n\n## Rotary on a galvo\n\n- **Pulses / turn** — the axis pulses for one full turn of the object. A PiBurn Grip 2 on these\n  boards is 48000 (12800 driver steps × 3.75 gearing, confirmed on the cup). With the laser's\n  rotary steps per turn and the rotary's gear ratio known, PiStudio suggests the figure.\n- **Band height** — the galvo cannot mark while the object turns, so a wrap is marked band by\n  band. Leave it empty and PiStudio chooses it from the diameter.\n\n## UV sources\n\nA UV source has no power setting: **Frequency** and **Q-pulse** set the energy of each layer.\n","06 Settings/01 Sounds.md":"---\nid: sounds\n---\n# Sounds\n\nPiStudio can play short sounds for things worth hearing. Settings → **Sounds**:\n\n- **Play sounds** — all sounds on or off.\n- **Volume**.\n- Each event on its own, with ▶ to hear it:\n\n| Event | When |\n|---|---|\n| Power dial sound | Rises as you turn the power up and holds at that level; fades when the pointer leaves the dial. It starts when you turn the dial, not when the pointer merely passes over it. Choose **Reactor** (a core spinning up — drone, turbine whine, a quickening pulse) or **Transformer** (a hum that grows louder and grittier). |\n| Job could not be sent | The laser did not take the job (not connected, busy, refused). |\n| Job stopped with an error | The laser reported a problem while running. |\n| Job sent | The laser took the job. |\n| Job finished | A job ran to the end. |\n| Laser connected | PiStudio reached the laser. |\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Sounds start after your first click in PiStudio — computers do not let programs play sound\n> before that.\n","06 Settings/02 Libraries and updates.md":"---\nid: libraries\n---\n# Libraries and updates\n\nThree things in PiStudio are kept up to date by LensDigital **without a new PiStudio**:\n\n| Library | What it holds |\n|---|---|\n| **Laser machines** | Lasers by type, make and model: the driver that runs each, its power, top speed, work area and rotary driver steps. |\n| **Rotaries** | Every rotary in the picker: chuck or roller, roller diameter, gear ratio, pictures, manual and shop links, and measured travel per rotation. |\n| **Help** | This manual. |\n\nSettings → **Libraries & help** shows the version of each in use.\n\n- **Check for updates** fetches newer libraries now. With **Check for updates when PiStudio\n  starts** ticked, it happens by itself, quietly.\n- **Load library file…** reads a `.pistudio-pack` file you were sent.\n- **Built-in** forgets an update and goes back to the library that came with PiStudio.\n\nA newer PiStudio brings newer libraries with it; an older download never replaces them.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> Your own devices, rotaries, cups and materials are never touched by an update: they are yours.\n","07 Troubleshooting/01 Cannot connect over USB.md":"---\nid: usb-driver\n---\n# Cannot connect over USB (xTool)\n\n**Check this first**, before cables and ports: on Windows the USB connection to an xTool F-series\nlaser needs the **RNDIS driver**, and Windows 11 no longer installs it by itself.\n\n## Why\n\nOver USB the laser is not a COM port — it is a small *network adapter* and answers at\n`201.234.3.1`. Without the driver Windows hears the laser (you get the USB chime) but never makes\nthe network connection, so no program can find it — PiStudio and xTool Studio alike.\n\n## You have this problem if\n\n- You hear the USB chime, but no laser is found.\n- **Device Manager** shows *RNDIS* or *Unknown device* under **Other devices**, with a yellow\n  triangle.\n\n## Fix it (about two minutes)\n\n1. Plug in the laser and switch it on. Open **Device Manager** (Win+X → Device Manager) and turn\n   on **View → Show hidden devices**.\n2. Find the device (usually under **Other devices**, named *RNDIS* or *Unknown device*).\n3. Right-click → **Update driver** → **Browse my computer for drivers** → **Let me pick from a\n   list of available drivers on my computer**.\n4. Choose **Network adapters** → Next.\n5. Manufacturer **Microsoft** → Model **Remote NDIS Compatible Device** (it may be called *USB\n   RNDIS Adapter* or *Remote NDIS based Internet Sharing Device*).\n6. Next → accept the warning → Finish.\n\nA new network adapter appears. In PiStudio, press **Scan again**, or connect to `201.234.3.1`.\n\n## Still nothing in Device Manager?\n\nThen it is physical, not the driver:\n\n- Use a **data** USB cable, not a charge-only one — the one that came with the laser is safest.\n- Plug straight into the computer, not through a hub.\n- Switch the laser on and let it finish starting before plugging in.\n\n> [!TIP]\n> Wi-Fi needs no driver: put the laser on your network once with xTool Studio, close Studio, and\n> use **Scan again** in PiStudio.\n\nOn a Mac, the laser shows up as a new adapter in System Settings → Network when it works over USB;\nif it does not, use Wi-Fi.\n","07 Troubleshooting/02 Wrong size or mirrored.md":"---\nid: wrong-size\ntitle: The engraving is the wrong size or mirrored\n---\n# The engraving is the wrong size or mirrored\n\n## Too long or too short around the cup\n\nThe design's size **along** the cup is right but it wraps too far or not far enough:\n\n1. **The diameter.** Measure the cup where the design sits and set that in Rotary setup. For a\n   **roller**, check the **roller diameter** instead — rubber wears a millimetre or two.\n2. **The rotary.** Is the rotary picked in Rotary setup the one on the laser?\n3. **Travel per rotation.** On an xTool, run the [calibration](calibration). On a Ruida or a\n   fiber galvo, check the **steps per rotation** — see\n   [Steps per rotation and gear ratio](steps-per-rotation) — and on a Ruida press\n   **Update machine**.\n\n## Mirrored\n\n- On a **Ruida** laser, check the **origin corner** of the device (Devices → your laser). A wrong\n  corner mirrors everything, exactly as in LightBurn.\n- Check **how the cup is mounted** in Rotary setup (held at the rim or at the base). Mounting the\n  cup the other way round turns the design end for end.\n\n## Upside down on the cup\n\nRotary setup → how the cup is mounted. PiStudio turns the job to match what you pick, so the\npicture there must match the cup on your rotary.\n","07 Troubleshooting/03 The laser stopped responding.md":"---\nid: laser-not-responding\n---\n# The laser stopped responding\n\n## An xTool that answers but does not move\n\nSometimes an xTool F-series laser keeps answering — it even says jobs finished — but the head and\nthe rotary never move. PiStudio notices when a job \"finishes\" much faster than it possibly could and\nshows a banner.\n\n**Power the laser off, wait a few seconds, and switch it back on.** That is the only thing that\nbrings it back; nothing sent from a computer does.\n\n## A job will not start\n\n- **Not connected** — click the connection chip in the top bar.\n- **The machine is still clearing** after a stop — wait a few seconds and try again.\n- **Busy** — another program (xTool Studio, LightBurn) may be connected. Close it.\n\n## A Ruida goes quiet during a job\n\nThat is normal: a Ruida controller often stops answering while it runs a job. PiStudio keeps\ntrying and carries on when it answers again.\n","images/device-form@2x.webp":"data:image/webp;base64,","images/dials@2x.webp":"data:image/webp;base64,"}}